Faxbot: NTT Data Connect calls, T.38 from the first INVITE (brief 99, item 2).

NTT's Hikari Denwa interface (音声利用IP通信網サービス 第2種タイプ2, version 13.1, pp. 20-21, read
10 October 2026) defines a Data Connect call (帯域確保型データ通信) as a call whose media is
not voice or video: an INVITE that offers m=image or m=application. A terminal that cannot
take it answers 488 Not Acceptable Here, with a Warning, and the caller may call again as
voice. NTT's own Hikari FAX tool (NTT East release, 12 November 2013) faxes that way: a Data
Connect fax first, an ordinary G3 call when the far end does not take one. NTT bills a Data
Connect call by its bandwidth (from ¥1.1 per 30 seconds) instead of the voice price (¥8.8 per
3 minutes). Asterisk opens every outgoing call as voice and moves to T.38 by re-INVITE, so it
could not place one.

With FAXBOT_DATA_CONNECT=yes on the channel (set by Faxbot's Originate, only for a number
whose machine takes Data Connect, over an NTT trunk with T.38 on):

1. chan_pjsip: the first INVITE carries one image stream instead of audio, as an outgoing
   T.38 re-INVITE would (the session starts in T38_LOCAL_REINVITE).
2. res_pjsip_t38: that offer carries res_fax's own parameters (T.38 version 0, 14,400 bit/s,
   transferredTCF, 400-octet IFPs, the endpoint's error correction), exactly ITU-T T.38
   Annex D's attributes, and a b=AS:64 line (RFC 4566), the bandwidth Faxbot prices it at.
   The channel is marked FAXBOT_DATA_CONNECT_RESULT=offered.
3. A 2xx whose image stream has a port and a UDPTL peer puts the session where an incoming
   T.38 offer puts it (T38_PEER_REINVITE), so SendFAX agrees the parameters exactly as
   ReceiveFAX does for one; the agreement now finds the image stream in the active media
   state when the pending one has none. Faxbot's UDPTL datagrams on the call are capped at
   160 octets (the 59-octet data packet at 14,400 bit/s and the redundancy that fits), so
   with UDP and IPv4 headers at spandsp's one datagram per 30 ms the session stays under
   50.2 kbit/s, inside what it declared. FAXBOT_DATA_CONNECT_RESULT=connected, which
   the dialplan's FaxResult event carries as DataConnect.
4. A 488 sets FAXBOT_DATA_CONNECT_RESULT=refused and sends a FaxDataConnect user event (the
   fax, the attempt, the code and the Warning, base64), so Faxbot calls again as an ordinary
   fax call for the same attempt, before any page (api/app/ami.py). Any other answer leaves
   the call as it is.

5. Receiving: Asterisk declines T.38 in a first INVITE. On an endpoint that takes Data
   Connect faxes (set_var=FAXBOT_DATA_CONNECT_ACCEPT=yes, which Faxbot renders for an NTT
   Hikari Denwa trunk with T.38 on), a T.38-only first INVITE puts the session where a
   peer's T.38 re-INVITE would (T38_PEER_REINVITE, without the rejection timer, since
   nothing is deferred), the answer carries res_fax's own parameters, and ReceiveFAX agrees
   them as it does for a re-INVITE. FAXBOT_DATA_CONNECT_IN=yes tells the dialplan to let the
   built-in engine take it.

Nothing changes a call that does not ask for Data Connect, or an endpoint that does not take it. Not yet run against NTT; proven
against a second Faxbot Asterisk that takes a T.38-only first INVITE and one that refuses it.

